---
// A public recipe explainer: what makes a canelé custardy vs cakey, from real ratio analysis.
//
// IT IS ON THE SITE'S OWN LAYOUT NOW (chooser: andrewshiau-grid-options, Q5/02 — "fold into the
// document"). It used to render from `Explainer.astro`, which carried a 307-line copy of an
// entire second design system: Graphik, twelve font sizes, weight-700 headings, uppercase
// labels, 2px rules, a 120px accent numeral, and a content axis 10px off the one every other
// page used. None of it loaded global.css, so none of the site's verification could see it.
// Now it is `Layout.astro` + `.pin`/`.feed` like every case study, and the only things unique to
// an explainer are the six devices global.css states for them (`.kicker`, `.sn`, `.idea`, `.num`,
// `.quiz`, `.foot`) plus this page's one diagram.
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const quiz = [
  { q: "You want a more custardy, softer interior. Which lever moves it most?",
    opts:["Add more sugar for moisture.","Lower the flour percentage toward 9–12%.","Use more whole eggs instead of yolks."], a:1 },
  { q: "A recipe is custardy but tastes hollow and flat. What’s most likely low?",
    opts:["Fat — butter, cream, or yolks.","Sugar.","Total liquid."], a:0 },
  { q: "You push flour above 15% of batter. What happens to the interior?",
    opts:["It gets richer and darker.","It turns firm, cakey, bread-like.","It collapses and stays raw."], a:1 },
  { q: "Why lean on egg yolks rather than whole eggs?",
    opts:["Yolks make it sturdier and easier to unmold.","Yolks add fat and emulsification for a silky, rich set.","Whites would make it sweeter."], a:1 },
];
---

<Layout
  title="What makes a canelé custardy — Andrew Shiau"
  description="An interactive explainer: the one ratio that decides whether a canelé is custardy or cakey."
  quiz={quiz}
>
  <div class="grid">
    <!-- THE PANEL, the same one every case study opens with. The explainer's old masthead — a
         three-dot mark in a bar with a 2px rule, plus an "Explainer · set in /swiss" caption —
         is deleted: the site has one identity block and this is it. `.pin` carries the kicker,
         the h1 and the ledger of what the piece is. -->
    <div class="pin">
      <p class="smark">Explainer</p>
      <h1>What makes a canelé custardy</h1>
      <div class="facts">
        <div class="frow">
          <div class="fm">What</div>
          <div class="ftx">Six popular canelé recipes, normalized to percentages of total batter
            weight, to find what actually decides the interior.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="frow">
          <div class="fm">Source</div>
          <div class="ftx">My own ratio analysis —
            <a href="https://halfcadence.github.io/recipes/a/canele-ratio-analysis"
              target="_blank" rel="noopener">halfcadence.github.io/recipes</a></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frow">
          <div class="fm">Back to</div>
          <div class="ftx"><a href="/work/explain/">Explain</a></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="splitrule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="feed">
      <div class="grid">
        <p class="dek c1-12">I normalized six popular canelé recipes to percentages of total
          batter weight to find what decides the interior. One number does most of the work.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- THE ONE IDEA. It was a 30px bold paragraph under a 3px accent rule; it is the body
           size in `--ink` with a group of air around it, which is the space scale doing the job
           the size jump did. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="idea c1-12">
          <span class="k">The one idea</span>
          <p>Flour, as a percent of batter, is the lever between custardy and cakey. Everything
            else is richness and colour.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Section heads are the site's hung head with a numeral in front. The `.rule` divs that
           used to open each section are gone with every other rule on the site — `--sect` of air
           is the break. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2><span class="sn">1</span>The number</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>Across the six recipes, the ones people call “custardy” sit at 9–14% flour. Push
              past 15% and the crumb sets up firm. The recipe I use lands at the high end and
              leans on fat to stay rich rather than dry.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="num c1-12">
          <span class="big">9–14%</span>
          <span class="lab">flour, as a share of total batter, for a custardy interior
            <span class="g">— above ~15% it turns cakey and bread-like</span></span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="scale c1-12">
          <div class="track">
            <div class="seg custard">more custardy · soft, fragile</div>
            <div class="seg sweet">15%</div>
            <div class="seg cakey">cakey · firm, bread-like</div>
          </div>
          <div class="ends"><span>flour 9%</span><span>flour 18%</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2><span class="sn">2</span>What each part does</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>Four levers, and only the first one changes the texture. The other three decide
              how it tastes.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="facts">
          <div class="frow"><div class="fm">Flour</div><div class="ftx">Structure. <b>More</b> = cakey and firm; <b>less</b> = custardy but fragile. The main lever.</div></div>
          <div class="frow"><div class="fm">Sugar</div><div class="ftx">Sweetness and caramelization. More = a darker shell; less = paler, less caramel.</div></div>
          <div class="frow"><div class="fm">Fat</div><div class="ftx">Butter, cream, yolk fat → richness. The lean recipes taste custardy but hollow. Fat is flavor.</div></div>
          <div class="frow"><div class="fm">Egg</div><div class="ftx">Yolks bind and emulsify for a rich set; more whole egg makes it sturdier but less silky.</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2><span class="sn">3</span>Check yourself</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>Four questions. The answer appears when you pick one.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid"><div class="quiz c1-12" id="quiz"></div></div>

      <div class="grid">
        <div class="foot c1-12">
          <a href="/work/explain/">Explain</a> ·
          <a href="/">andrewshiau.com</a>
          <span class="kb"> · Press <code>⌥G</code> for the grid</span>
          <span class="tou"> · Set on a 12-column grid</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style is:global>
  /* THE FLOUR SCALE — this page's one diagram, and the only bespoke CSS left on it.
     Rebuilt on the system: it was a 56px box with a 1px ink border, three uppercase 12px
     bold segments and a filled accent block for the 15% threshold. Now it is a row of three
     labels on one hairline, with the threshold marked by the accent — the one place colour is
     allowed to carry a fact here, because the threshold IS the point of the diagram.
     `28px` tall (one lead) so it sits on the page's baseline like everything else. */
  /* No vertical margin — the `.grid` wrapper owns the interval (see `.idea` in global.css for
     the measured reason). */
  .scale{margin:0}
  .scale .track{display:flex;border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-top:var(--tight)}
  .scale .seg{font-size:inherit;line-height:inherit;letter-spacing:normal;text-transform:none;
    font-weight:400;color:var(--dim)}
  .scale .custard{flex:0 0 46%}
  .scale .sweet{flex:0 0 8%;color:var(--accent);text-align:center}
  .scale .cakey{flex:1;text-align:right}
  .scale .ends{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;color:var(--faint);
    margin-top:var(--tight)}
  @media(max-width:1060px){
    .scale .track{display:block}
    .scale .seg{display:block;text-align:left}
    .scale .sweet{text-align:left}
  }
</style>
